    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-320871",
      "knowledge_point_id": "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically",
      "problem": "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-2,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t-2\\,⇒\\,t=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x+2$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+2)^{2}-4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}+4x.$",
